schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: A well-documented governance attack stole a significant amount from the Beanstalk DAO
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    DAOs can be manipulated by a governance attack, where a wealthy outsider
    buys enough voting tokens to take control; one well-documented attack
    "stole a significant amount" from a DAO called Beanstalk.
  evidence: >-
    Stated in is-dao-right.md's "Governance Attacks Are a Risk" section,
    with the article's References listing "DAO Governance Attacks, and How
    to Avoid Them" (a16z Crypto research, a16zcrypto.com) as a general
    source, not cited per-claim.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/is-dao-right.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used to warn small, tight-knit communities that governance-token
    concentration is a real risk, though the article notes small communities
    are less vulnerable than large token-weighted DAOs.
  uncertainty: >-
    The article gives no date or dollar figure for the Beanstalk attack and
    cites only a general a16z research link, not a specific article about
    the incident — the exact amount and mechanism were not independently
    verified for this ingestion.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/is-dao-right.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
